# The test source on the host: is every source frame captured once?
date:    2026-11-25, 21:05-21:45 UTC (captures); this log written during the host soak
host:    Linux Mint 22, 6.8.0-151-generic, gc573 (DKMS, srcversion 292FD1A2D4D0C4210A150E8) bound on the host
source:  GT 710 DVI-D-0 (NVIDIA DFP-0) -> GC573 HDMI IN; HDMI-0 (DFP-1, 1920x1200 @ 59.95 Hz) is the primary
         Cinnamon 6.2.10 / Muffin 6.2.0, NVIDIA 470.256.02; ffplay (FFmpeg 6.1.1, SDL 2.30.0)
capture: tests/gc573-aud -V -P (one line per captured frame: vb2 sequence and timestamp, the
         flash box's mean luma, the 16-bit counter of counter-graph.sh), each run under
         tools/testsrc/guard.py; counter and flash lines by tests/live-av.py's functions

## Report (coordinator, before this work)
A 60 s direct capture of plain avsync.sh: 47 single flash frames, 4 doubled, 1 torn and
8 missing, while the driver delivered 3597/3597 frames with no gaps. Suspected: Cinnamon's
compositor pacing the test window at the primary's 59.95 Hz.

## Root cause: the counter picture's frame times (tools/testsrc/counter-graph.sh)
The graph loops a PNG through the movie source, whose image2 stream has a 1/25 s time base;
setpts=N/(FPS)/TB then truncated the frame times to 40 ms steps. ffprobe of the graph:
  before (git show f37ce45~1:tools/testsrc/counter-graph.sh), 1080p60:
    0.000000 0.000000 0.000000 0.040000 0.040000 0.080000 0.080000 0.080000 0.120000 0.120000 0.160000 0.160000 0.200000 0.200000 1/25 
  after (settb=AVTB before setpts), 1080p60 and 59.94:
    0.000000 0.016666 0.033333 0.050000 0.066666 0.083333 0.100000 0.116666 1/1000000 
    0.000000 0.016683 0.033366 0.050050 0.066733 0.083416 0.100100 0.116783 1/1000000 
ffplay shows frames with equal times in bunches: on the card about 5 of every 6 source
frames, 10 repeated and 10 lost every second, evenly through the run. GL sync to DVI-D-0,
no vsync and SDL's software renderer leave it unchanged, so neither the GL swap nor the
compositor is the cause. Muffin 6.2.0 (src/compositor/meta-surface-actor-x11.c,
meta_surface_actor_x11_should_unredirect) unredirects an override-redirect, monitor-sized,
opaque top window whatever unredirect-fullscreen-windows says; the test window is all of
these (depth 24, the topmost viewable window), and Xorg and cinnamon used under 2 % CPU
while the player ran (the player about 20 % per busy thread), so the compositor most likely
does not draw it at all (not checked directly).

Plain avsync.sh takes its frames from a color source with r=FPS, whose time base is right;
its rarer misses come from the player's pacing against the audio clock (below).

