## Clocks
DVI-D-0's 1080p60 and the GPU's HDMI audio run at the same rate (+23 to +34 ppm against
CLOCK_MONOTONIC, within 1-3 ppm of each other in every run with audio), so avsync.sh, which
ffplay paces by the audio clock, holds its phase to the display: 300 s at 1080p60 without a
repeat or a lost frame. Elsewhere one clock gains on the other, and ffplay drops or repeats a
frame to keep audio and video together: show.sh --counter (system clock) about every 12 min,
avsync.sh at 1080p59.94 (display +13 ppm, audio +24) about every 25 min, and at 720p59.94
(the offset drifts 3.3 ms/min in tests/live.sh exact) about every 5 min. A session whose
phase starts near a vblank boundary can lose or repeat a frame or two while the audio clock
jitters across it: the plain 60 s run above, two flashes missing and a one-frame step at
31 s, then steady.

## Verification
tests/live.sh exact (af90921), local mode, 60 s each: show.sh --counter 1080p60,
avsync.sh --pattern 1080p60, 1080p59.94 and 720p59.94: every captured frame stepped the
counter by one, every flash captured once, no sequence gap (run before the soak; the
committed run log follows it).
